\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A rotation direction and a relativistic spectral theorem}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{With the graph, weights and cut fixed, the clock ratio in
\ref{fdip-0105} tends to #{1} as #{N} increases, since
#{1-\cos(2\pi /N)} tends to zero. On the circle a simultaneous rotation
has exactly [zero second variation](fdip-0106). This identifies the tangent to the
family of maximizing configurations.}

\p{A cited spectral input applies to a relativistic quantum field
theory with a Lorentz-invariant vacuum. Suppose a conserved current
generates an exact continuous global internal symmetry, and the variation
of a spinless field has nonzero vacuum expectation. The current-field
spectral distribution then has a nonzero contribution at zero mass,
giving massless excitations in the scalar channel.
\citet{§III, p. 968, equations (33)-(46)}{goldstone1962broken}
derive the spectral conclusion. In §IV, p. 969, they take the generation of the field variation by
the integrated current commutator as a hypothesis. The spatial
integral of the charge density need not converge as an operator. The finite
graph Hessian supplies neither that charge nor a relativistic
spectrum.}
